# arc-frontier.rq (loop the-arc-has-a-denominator, 2026-08-21) — WHAT TO DO NEXT, AND WHY.
#
# The residues that block an UNMET criterion, each with the rung and the criterion it blocks
# (spec 2026-08-20 §5, second bullet). This is the query that turns adjacency into strategy:
# §3.3's load-bearing edge is residue -> criterion -> rung, so a row here reads *closing R99
# matters because it unblocks a criterion of the `tab` rung* — not "R99 is old".
#
# GATE CLASSIFICATION (CLAUDE.md §8): **AXIOM / derivation, OPEN WORLD, evidence-positive.**
# Every row is grown from four triples that are all PRESENT: the rung, the criterion's rung
# key, its asserted prog:met, and the prog:blockedBy edge. There is NO negation in this query
# at all — not even a query-local one. It cannot infer anything from absence because it never
# looks at an absence: a criterion with no blocking edge simply contributes no row, which is
# the correct open-world reading (nobody has NAMED a blocker, which is not the same claim as
# "nothing blocks it"). The complementary question — *is this criterion ready to be worked?*
# — is arc-unblocked.rq's, and it is where the query-local closure lives.
#
# `prog:met false` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS NOT A DUPLICATE OF M8. The membrane already refuses
# a met criterion that carries prog:blockedBy (M8, tests/arc-shapes.ttl:130-134), so on an
# ADMITTED manifest this binding changes nothing. It is kept for the reason CLAUDE.md
# § Producer-side guards states: a derivation must be correct over the graph it is HANDED,
# and a query is handed graphs the membrane never saw — a fixture, a work-in-progress edit,
# a future manifest whose stale edge has not yet been caught. Delete the binding and a MET
# criterion's blocker appears on the frontier: the strip would then name work that is already
# done as the next thing to do, which is precisely the failure this query exists to avoid.
# (That deletion is this file's falsification arm; see the task-7 report.)
#
# "OPEN" IS A REGISTER FACT, AND IT IS NOT IN THIS GRAPH — stated plainly because spec §5
# words this query as *"the OPEN residues that block an unmet criterion"*. prog:blockedBy's
# values are PLAIN STRING LITERALS ("R43"), and the residue register is a markdown file that
# is not part of the graph at all — which is exactly why M7 reads it in procedural code
# (tests/test_arc_manifest.py, module docstring). So this query answers the graph half —
# *which residues block unmet criteria* — and open/closed is a caller-side qualifier a
# register-reading consumer may apply on top. M7 already guarantees every value here NAMES a
# real register row. MEASURED 2026-08-21: all 15 distinct residues reachable from
# prog:blockedBy are `open` in docs/superpowers/residues.md, so the two readings coincide
# exactly on today's manifest; the day one of them closes, its edge is stale and
# arc-unblocked.rq is the query that should have surfaced it.
#
# MEASURED against tests/arc-manifest.ttl, 2026-08-21: 20 rows, every one on the `tab` rung —
# and that is the honest answer, not a narrow query. prog:blockedBy is authored on `tab`
# alone (tests/arc-manifest.ttl:551 says so in the `dec` rung's own text); the other four
# rungs' unmet criteria are unblocked work, and they show up in arc-unblocked.rq instead.
PREFIX prog: <https://w3id.org/iladub/progress#>

SELECT ?residue ?rungKey ?criterion WHERE {
  # The rung, positively — same reason as arc-position.rq: a criterion naming a key no
  # prog:Rung declares is not evidence about any rung of the arc.
  ?rung a prog:Rung ;
        prog:rungKey ?rungKey .
  ?criterion a prog:Criterion ;
             prog:ofRung ?rungKey ;
             prog:met false ;
             prog:blockedBy ?residue .
}
ORDER BY ?residue ?rungKey ?criterion
